;
; Demo Reel 3 through the chipset, with the chipset on screen -- no keyboard.
;
; There is no input device on this machine yet (ISSUE-0076), so nothing can be
; typed at a Shell. Nothing here needs to be: the boot-test mechanism runs this
; from the Startup-Sequence, and the pieces it drives are all non-interactive.
;
; Order matters.
;
; The census first, because it is the cheap claim and it fails informatively:
; same background, same count, same checksum on both sides means the frame the
; chipset composed is the frame the guest can read. If that disagrees, nothing
; after it is worth reading.
;
; Then the overlay, in the background. DeniseView SHOW hands the HVS a plane
; pointing at the frame aperture and waits; the scaler re-reads that memory
; every frame, so the plane is live rather than a snapshot, and it keeps
; showing whatever Denise composes from here on. Run, because it does not
; return -- and with no keyboard there is no Ctrl-C to give it.
;
; Then the demo, which is the point.
;
Echo >DEBUG: "[demoreel-show] --- can the guest read what Denise drew?"
DeniseView >DEBUG: NOWINDOW

Echo >DEBUG: "[demoreel-show] --- putting the aperture on the display"
Run >NIL: <NIL: DeniseView SHOW

Echo >DEBUG: "[demoreel-show] --- what is on the volume"
List >DEBUG: DemoReel3/Slish DemoReel3/toram DemoReel3/Monument

Echo >DEBUG: "[demoreel-show] --- ToRAM (assigns, 1 MB check, tunes to RAM:)"
CD DemoReel3
Execute toram
Echo >DEBUG: "[demoreel-show] --- ToRAM returned"

Echo >DEBUG: "[demoreel-show] --- Slish"
Slish >DEBUG:
Echo >DEBUG: "[demoreel-show] --- Slish returned"
